In article <7710@pyr.gatech.EDU> cheung@pyr.gatech.EDU (S. Y. Cheung) writes:
>In article <1989Mar22.165854.7851@ux63.bath.ac.uk>, ma_mef@ux63.bath.ac.uk (Fletcher) writes:
[edited slightly]
>> I then hit H (shift-h) to move to the left side of the room... I was
>> blinded by a blast of light and I continued moving until I reached
>> the cockatrice corpse when I automatically tried to feel what was on
>> the floor, touched the corpse and died...
>> In 3.0 can the code be changed so that if *ANYTHING* happens while
>> you are doing a shifted-move (H,J,K,L etc) you stop. This seems more
>> sensible and in line with stopping searching or stopping opening tins
>> if attacked.
>This is not fair since by typing H,J, etc your intention is to move
>until you find something interesting. Because you are blind, detecting
>if something is interesting (e.g. whether there is something on the
>floor) is done by sense, i.e. touching the floor or walls. 

Remember that the original poster didn't _start_ moving with H while
blinded.  Wouldn't you consider being blinded by the flash of light to
be worth stopping for?  Consider that any other creature attacking
would have been cause enough to stop... I'm surprised, in fact, that
the blinding _didn't_ cause the player to stop.

Now if Fletcher started moving with "H" while blinded, then I'd say he
got what he deserved; but if he started moving, was blinded, kept
moving, and hit the cockatrice, I'd say that's a problem.


